            </contributor>
            <creation xml:id="creation_6066f796">
               <date isodate="1763" xml:id="date_9a213157">1763</date>
               <geogName xml:id="geogName_0b7f2224">Copenhagen,</geogName>
            </creation>
            <history xml:id="history_b03cdef7">
               <p xml:id="idm112">Following the end of the Seven Years' War in 1763, it was decided to celebrate the event officially on 28 June. The Cathedral of Copenhagen held a church service including music both before and after the sermon. Scheibe composed a cantata including two hymn texts by his acquaintance the royal Chaplain, <persName xml:id="idm113" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Johann Andreas Cramer</persName> ('Herrn Cramers Psalmen, Th. II'). It was performed on the same day at Frederiks tyske Kirke in another part of the capital (Christianshavn). For this event the cantata was introduced by a prelude for trumpets and kettledrums which was a tradition often employed when celebrating important events. On the following day, the concert was repeated in The Musical Society to a large audience and was so well performed that it received great applause. The cantata was briefly reviewed in the newspaper, <rend xml:id="idm114" fontstyle="italic">Kiøbenhavnske danske Post-Tidender</rend>, mentioning that the work 'was composed with great diligence, and that especially the poetry shows how the members of the Society with sincerity and patriotic feeling have taken the celebration of this grand festivity into solemn consideration'. Only the text book has survived.</p>
            </history>
